title 04 | Every layer, every kind of hop, and the return journey [overview stages 5-7]
box "CLIENT HOST" #F8FAFC
  participant "HTTP + TLS\nclient process" as C #DBEAFE
  participant "Socket API\nOS TCP + IP" as O #EDE9FE
  participant "Client NIC\nlink MAC + physical PHY" as CN #DCFCE7
end box
participant "Local Ethernet\nswitch / optional Wi-Fi AP" as L #DCFCE7
participant "Gateway G\nLAN and WAN interfaces" as G #FFEDD5
participant "ISP / transit\nrouter R1 ... Rn" as R #FFEDD5
participant "Destination\nedge router E" as E #FFEDD5
box "SERVER HOST" #F8FAFC
  participant "Server NIC\nlink MAC + physical PHY" as SN #DCFCE7
  participant "OS IP + TCP\nsocket API" as SO #EDE9FE
  participant "TLS + HTTP\nserver process" as S #DBEAFE
end box

note over C,S
  READ FIRST: handshake is already complete [03]. Follow one request packet, then its response.
  Many packets may be needed; this trace simplifies their repetition, not the layers.
  Baseline access: Ethernet via a switch. Wi-Fi differences are in a separate note below.
  C=192.168.1.10:51514; gateway LAN=192.168.1.1; server=203.0.113.20:443.
  Example optional NAT: gateway public endpoint=198.51.100.10:62000.
  MAC_C, MAC_G_LAN, etc. are symbolic link addresses. Remote IPs use documentation ranges.
end note

== 5a | Application, security, socket, and transport ==
C -> C: [Application] Serialize GET /hello HTTP/1.1\nHost: www.example.com; header terminator
C -> C: [TLS above TCP] Encrypt/authenticate plaintext\nTLS record = header + protected content + tag
C -> O: [Socket API] send/write encrypted bytes\nLocal API boundary: application -> transport service
O -> O: [Transport] Buffer bytes; segment within MSS / path limit\nTCP header: ports, sequence/ACK, window, checksum
note over C,O #EDE9FE
  Buffering, offload, segmentation, and scheduling are implementation dependent.
  A TLS record can span TCP segments; a segment can contain record pieces.
  TCP receive reconstructs a stream, not application message boundaries.
end note

== 5b | Network layer chooses the next hop ==
O -> O: [Network] Construct IP packet carrying a TCP segment\nSrc=192.168.1.10; Dst=203.0.113.20; example TTL=64
O -> O: Route lookup: destination is outside 192.168.1.0/24\nChoose gateway 192.168.1.1 and outgoing NIC
note over O,G #FFEDD5
  Destination IP stays the remote server's IP.
  Next hop is a local forwarding decision; it is not the HTTP destination.
  More specific routes can override the default gateway.
end note

== 5c | Link layer resolves the local next-hop address ==
opt Gateway neighbor entry absent from ARP cache
  O -> CN: ARP request for gateway 192.168.1.1
  CN -> L: [Physical] Transmit frame as encoded electrical signals\n[Link] Ethernet broadcast destination FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF
  L -> L: Recover bits; learn source MAC_C -> ingress port\nFlood broadcast to other eligible ports in the VLAN
  L -> G: Broadcast ARP request: who has 192.168.1.1?
  G --> L: ARP reply: 192.168.1.1 is MAC_G_LAN
  L --> CN: Unicast reply toward MAC_C
  CN --> O: Cache gateway IP -> MAC_G_LAN
end
note over CN,G #DCFCE7
  ARP is local link traffic, not an IP packet forwarded across routers.
  For an on-subnet server, resolve that server's MAC instead.
end note

== 5d | First link: frame, bits, and physical signals ==
O -> CN: [Network -> Link] Queue IP packet for MAC_G_LAN
CN -> CN: [Link] Build Ethernet frame:\nDst MAC_G_LAN | Src MAC_C | EtherType IPv4\nIP packet | padding if needed | FCS
note over C,CN #DBEAFE
  Conceptual nesting for this data packet:
  Ethernet header [ IP header [ TCP header [ TLS ciphertext bytes ] ] ] FCS
  Original HTTP bytes are inside authenticated encryption.
  Headers shown do not imply one whole HTTP message fits in one frame.
end note
CN -> CN: [Physical] Serialize frame transmission;\nPHY encodes symbols for negotiated Ethernet link
CN -> L: Copper: electrical signal waveform over the cable\nPreamble / synchronization precede frame bits
L -> L: [Physical Rx] Recover timing, symbols, bits\n[Link Rx] Validate frame FCS; reject corrupt frame
L -> L: Learn source MAC; look up destination MAC\nKnown unicast -> chosen port; unknown unicast -> flood
L -> G: [Physical Tx] New electrical transmission on egress cable\nEthernet Src=MAC_C, Dst=MAC_G_LAN
note over L #DCFCE7
  A normal switch bridges frames within the VLAN.
  It preserves these endpoint MAC addresses and the IP packet;
  it does not decrement IP TTL for ordinary switching.
  Physical transmission restarts on the outgoing port.
end note

== 5e | Gateway: physical receive -> link receive -> IP forward ==
G -> G: Ingress PHY recovers bits; MAC checks FCS\nRemove incoming Ethernet header/trailer; inspect IP header
G -> G: [Network] Longest-prefix lookup -> WAN next hop\nDecrement TTL 64 -> 63; update IPv4 header checksum
opt Gateway performs example outbound NAT / port translation
  G -> G: Map 192.168.1.10:51514 -> 198.51.100.10:62000\nRewrite source IP/port; adjust affected IP/TCP checksums\nKeep mapping for matching return traffic
end
G -> G: [Link] Obtain WAN next-hop link address if needed\nBuild new frame: Src=MAC_G_WAN; Dst=MAC_R1_IN\n[Physical] Outgoing PHY generates link signals
G -> R: Example fiber link: encoded optical transmission\nCarries IP/TCP packet containing TLS ciphertext

== 5f | Repeat forwarding across the Internet ==
loop Each illustrated ISP / transit router along the selected path
  R -> R: Rx PHY -> recover bits; Rx link -> validate/extract IP\nIP: longest-prefix lookup, decrease TTL, check MTU\nSwitching fabric -> egress queue -> new link frame -> Tx PHY
end
note over R #FFEDD5
  ROUTING CONTROL PLANE: OSPF / other IGP inside an AS;
  BGP between ASes advertises reachability and policy.
  Selected routes populate forwarding tables in advance.
  DATA PLANE: each arriving packet uses the current table;
  it does not run BGP negotiation for every HTTP request.
  AS = autonomous system; IGP = interior gateway protocol.
end note
note over G,E #FFEDD5
  At each routed boundary: discard incoming link encapsulation and create outgoing encapsulation.
  Link technology can change: Ethernet, provider links, radio, etc.; not every link uses MAC/ARP.
  NAT aside, IP endpoints stay constant; TTL decreases and IPv4 checksum changes.
  Ordinary transit forwarding does not decrypt TLS or reconstruct HTTP.
end note
R -> E: Last transit link: packet toward 203.0.113.20

== 5g | Destination edge and last link ==
E -> E: Rx PHY -> Link -> IP; find directly attached server subnet\nDecrease TTL; resolve server neighbor if cache misses
E -> E: Build final Ethernet frame:\nSrc=MAC_E_LAN; Dst=MAC_SERVER; unchanged IP destination
E -> SN: Example last copper link: encoded electrical signals
SN -> SN: PHY recovers symbols/bits; MAC validates FCS\nAccept local destination frame; extract IP packet
SN -> SO: [Link -> Network] Deliver IP packet to host stack

== 6 | Server receives upward through all layers ==
SO -> SO: [IP] Validate header / destination / protocol;\nreassemble IP fragments if this example path produced any
SO -> SO: [TCP] Verify checksum; identify connection;\nreorder/deduplicate bytes; update receive state and ACKs
SO --> S: [Socket] recv/read supplies available ordered ciphertext bytes
S -> S: [TLS] Assemble records; authenticate/decrypt\n[HTTP] Parse framing; dispatch /hello handler
S -> S: Prepare HTTP/1.1 200 OK; Content-Length: 5; body Hello
note over SO,S #EDE9FE
  TCP ACK generation can occur before TLS decryption or handler execution.
  A receive call can return fewer bytes than a TLS record or HTTP message.
end note

== 7 | Response descends the server stack and returns ==
S -> S: HTTP serialize -> TLS record protection
S -> SO: Socket send/write: response ciphertext
SO -> SO: TCP segment: Src port=443, Dst port=62000 with NAT\nIP: Src=203.0.113.20, Dst=198.51.100.10\nChoose response route and next hop
SO -> SN: IP packet -> Ethernet frame toward MAC_E_LAN
SN --> E: Physical response transmission; Link -> IP at E
E -> E: IP lookup; TTL decrement; new outgoing link encapsulation
E --> R: Outgoing response signals carrying frame/packet
R -> R: Repeat Rx PHY -> Link -> IP -> Link -> Tx PHY\nReturn path may use different routers
R --> G: Response packet addressed to gateway public IP
opt The request used NAT and the return mapping exists
  G -> G: Reverse mapping of destination endpoint:\n198.51.100.10:62000 -> 192.168.1.10:51514\nAdjust affected checksums
end
G -> G: Route to client LAN; decrease TTL; use cached / resolved MAC_C\nNew Ethernet frame: Src=MAC_G_LAN, Dst=MAC_C
G --> L: Physical transmission on LAN cable
L --> CN: Bridge response frame; new physical transmission to client
CN -> CN: PHY recover bits -> Link validate/extract IP
CN --> O: Deliver IP packet to host stack
O -> O: IP -> TCP checksum/demux/reorder -> socket buffer
O --> C: Socket recv -> TLS verify/decrypt -> HTTP response parse
C -> C: Application consumes Hello
note over SO,G #FFEDD5
  Without NAT: response destination is 192.168.1.10:51514 in this private routed example.
  Addresses must be routable within that chosen network; skip both translation steps.
  The pictured return uses the same device roles only to keep the drawing readable.
end note

== Depth notes | Wi-Fi, physical media, MTU, and packet loss ==
note over CN,L #DCFCE7
  WI-FI ACCESS ALTERNATIVE:
  Associate with AP; use 802.11 MAC frames and radio PHY.
  In a typical infrastructure uplink, receiver address is the AP/BSSID;
  other address fields identify the original source and bridged destination.
  AP bridges payload into Ethernet; it does not route merely because it is an AP.
  Shared radio access: CSMA/CA contention/backoff; many unicast frames get link ACK/retry.
  A Wi-Fi link ACK is local and is different from an end-to-end TCP ACK.
end note
note over CN,E #FEF3C7
  PHYSICAL LAYER ON EACH LINK:
  Tx: frame bits -> encoding/modulation -> electrical / optical / radio signal.
  Rx: synchronization, symbol recovery, decoding -> frame bits for the MAC.
  Copper: voltage/current signaling; fiber: modulated light; Wi-Fi: radio modulation, often OFDM.
  Bit rate controls serialization time; distance and medium control propagation time.
  Link checks and optional local retries do not guarantee end-to-end application delivery.
  NIC = network interface controller; MAC = media access control; PHY = physical transceiver.
end note
note over O,E #FFEDD5
  MTU bounds an outgoing IP packet's size; TCP MSS bounds segment payload.
  IPv4 DF=1 + packet too large: discard and report ICMP fragmentation-needed when allowed.
  Path MTU discovery lets the sender reduce packet sizes; IPv4 DF=0 may allow fragmentation.
  IPv6 routers do not fragment; ICMPv6 Packet Too Big informs the sender [06].
  Corrupt frames, expired TTL, queue overflow, or unreachable routes may drop packets [05].
end note
legend left
  |= Color |= Role |
  |<#DBEAFE> Application | HTTP, DNS, application logic |
  |<#FCE7F3> Security | TLS above TCP; SSL is the historical name |
  |<#EDE9FE> Transport | TCP; sockets are a local API boundary |
  |<#FFEDD5> Network | IP addressing and router forwarding |
  |<#DCFCE7> Link | Ethernet / Wi-Fi framing and local delivery |
  |<#FEF3C7> Physical | Electrical, optical, or radio signals |
  Solid arrows: actions / exchanges. Dashed arrows: replies / completions.
  Arrow style does not imply a different delivery guarantee.
endlegend

@enduml
